# SYNOPSIS

```ndb
match=acpi hid=PNP0B00 program=/boot/bin/drv-rtc-cmos clock
```

# DESCRIPTION

`drv-rtc-cmos` reads the PC's CMOS real-time clock once, at boot, and tells devmgr(8) the time, from which devmgr sets the kernel's wall clock.
It serves no files and posts nothing.

It is the first driver started from an ACPI device.
bus-acpi(8) reports each device present in the firmware's ACPI namespace with its hardware ID and its `_CRS` resources; devmgr matches the clock's ID, `PNP0B00`, against the manifest in `/boot/drv/rtc-cmos.ndb` shown above, and starts the driver with exactly those resources, unless one is the kernel's or another driver's.
That is its I/O ports, the handle `io0` with a record `io=0 base=... size=...` (the index and data ports 0x70 and 0x71 at least), and its interrupt, `irq0`, which it does not use.
The manifest's `clock` gives it besides a channel, `devmgr`, to say the time on, and, when the FADT names a CMOS century register, a record `fadt century=`<reg>.
It takes no arguments.
It is x86_64's alone: aarch64 has no I/O ports, and QEMU's aarch64 tables list no clock.

To read the clock it waits while the clock says an update is under way, reads seconds, minutes, hours, day, month, year and the century register, and reads them again until two readings agree, ten times at most, so an update between the reads cannot leave a mix of two seconds.
It takes the registers as BCD or binary, and the hours as 12- or 24-hour, as status register B says.
Without a century register the century is the 21st.
The clock is taken to keep UTC.

It sends devmgr one message, `VX_CLOCK_REPORT` of `lib/vx-driver/clockproto.h`: the time in nanoseconds since 1970 and the monotonic clock's reading when it was read.
devmgr, which holds the root Resource the driver does not, adds the time since and sets the wall clock with `clock_set`, then says what it set.
The driver then waits forever, holding the clock's ports.

# EXAMPLES

Under QEMU with its clock started at 2030-01-02T03:04:05, as the rtc test runs it:

```text
devmgr: \_SB.PCI0.SF8.RTC PNP0B00: /boot/bin/drv-rtc-cmos
devmgr: started drv-rtc-cmos
devmgr: the wall clock is 2030-01-02 03:04:06 UTC, from /boot/bin/drv-rtc-cmos
drv-rtc-cmos: the clock says 1893553446 s since 1970
```

