# DIAGNOSTICS

It prints `drv-rtc-cmos: the clock says` and the seconds since 1970 once it has told devmgr.
It exits `no CMOS ports` without its ports, its channel or a range of at least two ports; `cannot use the CMOS ports` if they cannot be mapped; `the clock's registers make no time` for a reading out of range; and `cannot tell devmgr the time` if the message cannot be sent.
devmgr starts it again after an exit, up to five starts in all.

# BUGS

The clock is read once: the wall clock is set at boot, as a step, and never read again, slewed or written back to the clock, and nothing sets the clock.
The interrupt and alarms are unused.
A clock that keeps local time, as one set by Windows does, gives a wall clock off by the zone's offset.
Nothing orders services after the wall clock is set: one started before it stamps times in 1970.
The wait for an update to end gives up after a million reads and reads anyway; a day is checked only to be 1 to 31, not against its month.
There is no clock on aarch64, where dates read as 1970: there is no driver for its PL031.
